[38;2;124;124;124mSince $Y$ is compactly generated Hausdorff, a subset $A \subseteq Y$ is closed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mif and only if $A \cap K$ is closed in $K$ for every compact $K \subseteq Y$. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mLet $C \subseteq X$ be closed. For any compact $K \subseteq Y$, $f^{-1}(K)$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact (by properness), so $C \cap f^{-1}(K)$ is compact (closed subset of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact). Its image $f(C \cap f^{-1}(K)) = f(C) \cap K$ is compact (continuous [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mimage of compact), hence closed in $Y$ (since $Y$ is Hausdorff), and thus closed[0m[38;2;124;124;124min $K$. Since this holds for all compact $K$, $f(C)$ is closed in $Y$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 2: The fiber $f^{-1}(y)$ is finite for every $y \in Y$.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $\{y\}$ is compact and $f$ is proper, $f^{-1}(y)$ is compact. Since $f$ is[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma local homeomorphism, for each $x \in f^{-1}(y)$ there is an open neighborhood [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$U$ of $x$ with $f|_U$ injective, so $U \cap f^{-1}(y) = \{x\}$. Thus $f^{-1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(y)$ is discrete. A compact discrete space is finite.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 3: $f$ is surjective (assuming $X \neq \emptyset$).**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $f$ is a local homeomorphism, it is an open map, so $f(X)$ is open in $Y$.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mSince $f$ is closed (Step 1) and $X$ is closed in $X$, $f(X)$ is closed in $Y$. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSince $Y$ is connected and $f(X)$ is nonempty (as $X \neq \emptyset$), $f(X) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mY$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 4: The fiber cardinality $|f^{-1}(y)|$ is locally constant.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFix $y_0 \in Y$ and write $f^{-1}(y_0) = \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\}$. Since $X$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHausdorff, choose pairwise disjoint open neighborhoods $U_i$ of $x_i$. Since $f$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis a local homeomorphism, shrink each $U_i$ so that $f|_{U_i}: U_i \to f(U_i)$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis a homeomorphism onto an open set. Set $V' = \bigcap_{i=1}^n f(U_i)$, an open [0m[38;2;124;124;124mneighborhood of $y_0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe set $X \setminus \bigcup_i U_i$ is closed in $X$, so $f(X \setminus [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\bigcup_i U_i)$ is closed in $Y$ (Step 1). Since $y_0 \notin f(X \setminus [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\bigcup_i U_i)$ (all preimages of $y_0$ lie in $\bigcup_i U_i$), the set $V = V'[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\setminus f(X \setminus \bigcup_i U_i)$ is an open neighborhood of $y_0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor any $y \in V$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $y \in V' \subseteq f(U_i)$ for each $i$, so each $U_i$ contains at least one [0m[38;2;124;124;124mpreimage of $y$: $|f^{-1}(y)| \geq n$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $y \notin f(X \setminus \bigcup_i U_i)$, so $f^{-1}(y) \subseteq \bigcup_i [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mU_i$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Since the $U_i$ are disjoint and $f|_{U_i}$ is injective, $|f^{-1}(y) \cap [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mU_i| = 1$ for each $i$, giving $|f^{-1}(y)| = n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 5: $f$ is a covering map.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $Y$ is connected and $|f^{-1}(y)|$ is locally constant (Step 4), it is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconstant, say $n$, for all $y \in Y$. For each $y_0 \in Y$, construct $V$ and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$U_1, \ldots, U_n$ as in Step 4. Then:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $f^{-1}(V) \subseteq \bigcup_i U_i$ (from the construction of $V$).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $W_i := f^{-1}(V) \cap U_i = (f|_{U_i})^{-1}(V)$ is open and maps [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhomeomorphically onto $V$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The $W_i$ are pairwise disjoint (since the $U_i$ are).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $f^{-1}(V) = \bigsqcup_{i=1}^n W_i$.[0m

